<%- include('partials/head') %>
<%- include('partials/header') %>

<main id="main">

  <%- include('partials/page-hero', {
    heroImage: '/img/hero-projects.webp',
    eyebrow: 'Real results',
    eyebrowIcon: 'brush',
    heroTitle: 'Before & After Cleaning Projects Across Kansas City',
    heroIntro: 'Every project below is a real job with a real address and a real timeline. We publish the scope, the crew size, what we found on arrival and what the client said afterwards — because a gallery of cropped close-ups tells you nothing.',
    heroPoints: ['Documented before and after images', 'Real scopes, crews and durations', 'Residential and commercial work', 'Client quotes on every project'],
    breadcrumbs: [{ name: 'Projects', href: '/projects' }]
  }) %>

  <!-- ============ INTRO ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose prose--lead reveal">
        <p>
          Cleaning is one of the few services where the result is genuinely visible, and yet most company
          websites show a handful of stock photographs of spotless kitchens that no one on the team has
          ever touched. We take the opposite approach. Every project here was photographed on site, and we
          show the before alongside the after, because the before is where the actual work is.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also publish the unflattering details. The 1920s bungalow that had not been professionally
          cleaned in four years. The new build with paint overspray on fourteen windows and grout haze
          across 900 square feet of tile, handed over on a fixed date that could not move. The downtown
          loft with a property manager known for thorough inspections. Those are the jobs where a cleaning
          company either performs or does not, and they are the ones worth showing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ PROJECTS GRID ============ -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section__head section__head--center">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal"><span class="eyebrow__icon"><%- icon('list', 15) %></span> Case studies</p>
        <h2 class="section__title reveal">Browse Our Cleaning Projects</h2>
        <p class="section__lede reveal"><%= projects.length %> detailed case studies from across the Kansas City metro, covering residential, commercial and construction work.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="projects-grid projects-grid--full stagger">
        <% projects.forEach(function (p) { %>
          <article class="project-card reveal">
            <a class="project-card__link" href="/projects/<%= p.slug %>">
              <div class="project-card__media">
                <img class="project-card__after" src="<%= p.after %>" alt="<%= p.title %> after cleaning by Zenvorali Cleaning" width="720" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                     srcset="<%= srcset(p.after) %>" sizes="(min-width: 1040px) 32vw, (min-width: 640px) 48vw, 92vw">
                <img class="project-card__before" src="<%= p.before %>" alt="<%= p.title %> before cleaning" width="720" height="500" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                <span class="project-card__tag">Before &amp; After</span>
                <span class="project-card__cat"><%= p.category %></span>
              </div>
              <div class="project-card__body">
                <p class="project-card__loc"><%- icon('map', 14) %> <%= p.location %></p>
                <h3 class="project-card__title"><%= p.title %></h3>
                <p class="project-card__text"><%= p.summary %></p>
                <ul class="project-card__stats">
                  <% p.stats.forEach(function (st) { %>
                    <li><strong><%= st.value %></strong><span><%= st.label %></span></li>
                  <% }); %>
                </ul>
                <span class="project-card__more">Read the case study <%- icon('arrowRight', 16) %></span>
              </div>
            </a>
          </article>
        <% }); %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ BY SERVICE ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="section__head section__head--center">
        <p class="eyebrow reveal"><span class="eyebrow__icon"><%- icon('list', 15) %></span> Explore by service</p>
        <h2 class="section__title reveal">Looking for a Specific Type of Work?</h2>
      </header>
      <div class="related-grid stagger">
        <% services.forEach(function (s) { %>
          <article class="related-card reveal">
            <a class="related-card__link" href="/services/<%= s.slug %>">
              <span class="related-card__icon"><%- icon(s.icon, 26) %></span>
              <h3 class="related-card__title"><%= s.name %></h3>
              <p class="related-card__text"><%= s.tagline %></p>
              <span class="related-card__more">View service <%- icon('arrowRight', 16) %></span>
            </a>
          </article>
        <% }); %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ READING A CASE STUDY ============ -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <h2 class="section__title">What to Look For in a Cleaning Case Study</h2>
        <p>
          Most cleaning companies show you a photograph of someone else's clean room and leave it at that.
          A photograph on its own tells you almost nothing, because you cannot see the scope behind it,
          how long the job took, what condition it started in or what happened when something went wrong.
          These case studies are written around those details instead, because the detail is the only part
          that helps you decide whether to hire us.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you compare our projects against another company's gallery, these are the four things worth
          checking. They are also the four things we deliberately include in every write-up here.
        </p>
        <ul class="tick-list">
          <li><strong>Starting condition, not just the finished room.</strong> Every project below has a genuine before-and-after pair. A deep clean of a well-kept home and a deep clean of a house left four years are entirely different jobs at very different prices, and only the "before" image shows which one you are looking at.</li>
          <li><strong>Scope in writing.</strong> Each case study lists the actual work carried out line by line — trim hand-wiped, grout steam-treated, overspray removed from fourteen windows. If a company cannot list what it did, it cannot quote what it will do for you.</li>
          <li><strong>Time on site and crew size.</strong> Duration tells you what the job really costs to deliver. A price that looks low against a two-day, three-person scope is not a bargain, it is a shortcut you will discover later.</li>
          <li><strong>What happened afterwards.</strong> The outcome matters more than the reveal. A deposit returned, an inspection passed with zero notes, a contract renewed — those are the results a client actually buys.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">Why We Publish the Difficult Ones</h2>
        <p>
          It would be easy to publish only the dramatic transformations, because those are the most
          flattering. We include the jobs with real constraints instead: a downtown loft where the service
          lift was the bottleneck, a new build handed over with two days' notice, a fitness studio with a
          5&nbsp;AM opening that left a narrow overnight window.
        </p>
        <p>
          Those constraints are usually what a prospective client is genuinely worried about — access,
          timing, working around trades or staff, keeping noise and chemicals away from customers. A case
          study where nothing was difficult says nothing about whether we could handle your property.
        </p>

        <h2 class="section__title section__title--spaced">How to Get a Quote Like These</h2>
        <p>
          Every project here started the same way: a message, a walkthrough, and a flat-rate quote in
          writing the same day. We do not require a contract for residential work, and commercial accounts
          can be trialled one visit at a time. If you send photographs of the space along with your
          request, we can often quote without needing to visit at all — which is why most projects on this
          page were scheduled within a week of the first enquiry.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

<!-- ============ PROJECT HUB PROSE ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container container--narrow prose">
      <h2 class="section__title">What These Case Studies Are Actually For</h2>
      <p>
        A portfolio page on a cleaning website usually does one of two things. Either it shows a
        photograph of a spotless kitchen and asks you to take it on faith, or it lists a job title and
        a suburb and leaves you to guess. Neither helps you decide whether the company on the page can
        handle <em>your</em> property. So we have written ours as working documents rather than
        advertisements. Each case study below records the property type, the state it was in when we
        arrived, what we were specifically asked to solve, how long it took, and what it cost in the
        bands you will see on our <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a>. Where a job went sideways, we
        say so. Where a client's expectations turned out to be unrealistic, we say that too.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last point matters more than it sounds. Cleaning is a trade where the gap between what a
        customer imagines is possible and what a space will actually yield in a given number of hours
        is usually the whole source of friction. A vacant rental that has sat through a Kansas City
        summer with the air conditioning off will have grout and window track problems that no
        single visit fully solves — not because the crew was lazy, but because mineral scale and
        mildew staining build in layers. A post-construction handover where the painters finished
        drywall sanding the day before is a different job than one where the site was swept and
        wiped for a week before we arrived. Reading a few of these in sequence calibrates your
        expectations, which makes the quoting conversation shorter and the result less surprising.
      </p>

      <h3>The Variables That Change Every Quote</h3>
      <p>
        Almost every cleaning quote in the Kansas City metro comes down to six variables. Square
        footage sets the floor. The number of bathrooms and kitchens sets the ceiling, because those
        rooms carry most of the labour. The condition baseline — how long since a proper clean, and
        whether anything has been allowed to set — determines which service is appropriate in the
        first place. Access matters more than people expect: a third-floor walk-up, a building with
        a single service elevator that must be reserved, a gated community with a gate code that
        changes, or a commercial site that can only be worked between certain hours will all pull
        the price up because the crew's productive window shrinks. Occupancy matters: an empty home
        can be worked at full speed, while an occupied one requires moving around possessions,
        pets and people. And finally, the finish level you want — a standard maintenance result,
        a full deep clean, or a handover standard where someone will run a finger along a baseboard
        — changes the labour multiplier. Those same six variables explain almost every difference
        between the case studies below.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you can answer those six questions about your property before you contact us, you will
        get a quote that holds. If you cannot, send photographs. Photographs resolve the condition
        baseline instantly and let us quote the right service rather than the safest one, which is
        often a difference of several hundred dollars on a move-out.
      </p>

      <h3>Why We Publish Prices Alongside the Work</h3>
      <p>
        An enormous amount of consumer frustration in this industry comes from companies that will
        not name a number until they have you on the phone. We find that approach wastes everyone's
        time. Our pricing page publishes the actual bands we work in for each service, and the case
        studies here say which band each project landed in and why. A 1,900 square foot Overland Park
        ranch with three bathrooms and a recurring biweekly schedule is a completely different
        economics problem than a 900 square foot downtown loft cleaned once a month. Publishing both
        numbers lets you sanity-check whether we are the right company for your property before you
        spend time on a walkthrough, and it lets you compare a quote from us against a quote from
        anyone else on an apples-to-apples basis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two practical notes about comparing quotes. First, ask whether the number is flat or hourly.
        A flat quote protects you from a crew that works slowly; an hourly one does not. Second,
        ask what is <em>not</em> included. Inside appliances, inside cabinets, interior windows,
        garage sweeping, exterior glass above ground level and stain treatment are the six items
        most often quietly excluded from a headline price. We list them explicitly on every quote,
        included or not, so nothing arrives as a surprise on the day.
      </p>

      <h3>How to Read a Case Study Here</h3>
      <p>
        Each project page follows the same structure. The summary box gives you property type,
        location, service and turnaround at a glance. The narrative section explains what we found
        when we arrived and what we decided to do about it. The scope list is the actual work order
        the crew worked from — not a marketing summary of it. The lessons section records what the
        job taught us, including the parts we would do differently. The FAQ section handles the
        questions that project specifically tends to raise from people with similar properties.
        And the comparable-work section points you at the nearest equivalent project if your
        situation is close but not identical.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you read two or three of these and still are not sure which service applies to you, that
        is normal — and it is exactly what the walkthrough is for. We would rather spend fifteen
        minutes looking at your space and then tell you the honest answer, including telling you
        that a cheaper service will get you the result you want, than sell you the most expensive
        thing on the menu. Several of the case studies below record precisely that conversation
        happening.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%- include('partials/cta', {
    ctaEyebrow: 'Your property next',
    ctaTitle: 'Want a Result Like These?',
    ctaText: 'Send us photographs of the space and we will quote it flat, in writing, with realistic timing. Most projects can be scheduled within a week.'
  }) %>
</main>

<%- include('partials/footer') %>
<button class="to-top" id="toTop" type="button" aria-label="Back to top"><%- icon('chevronDown', 22) %></button>
